---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import InterestCalculator from '@components/islands/InterestCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('finance', 'interest-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'What is the difference between simple and compound interest?', answer: 'Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal, so it stays flat. Compound interest is calculated on the principal plus previously earned interest, so it grows faster over time. This calculator shows both side by side.' },
  { question: 'How does compounding frequency change my interest?', answer: 'More frequent compounding — daily versus monthly versus annually — adds interest to the balance sooner, so it starts earning interest sooner. The effect is small at low rates and larger over long periods.' },
  { question: 'Which type applies to my account?', answer: 'Most savings accounts and investments compound (often daily or monthly). Some short-term loans and bonds use simple interest. Check your account terms.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout calculator={calc} reviewedOn="2026-07-18" intro="Calculate the interest earned on a deposit and see exactly how much extra compounding adds compared with simple interest." faqs={faqs}>
  <InterestCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Simple vs. compound, side by side</h2>
    <p>Enter a principal, rate and time, and the calculator shows the interest under both methods plus the <strong>compounding advantage</strong> — the extra you earn purely because interest earns interest. Over long horizons, that gap becomes substantial.</p>
    <h2>Why compounding wins</h2>
    <p>With simple interest, your $10,000 at 5% earns a flat $500 every year. With compounding, each year's interest joins the balance and earns its own interest, so the yearly amount keeps rising. This is the engine behind long-term saving — explore it further with the <a href="/finance/compound-interest-calculator">compound interest calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>This is general educational information, not financial advice.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
